@* The hand-written-RenderFragment side of the §7.1 dynamic-content comparison. Must render
   frame-for-frame identically to OpaqueContentView; Program.cs refuses to benchmark if it does not.

   @DynamicContent() reaches the same RenderTreeBuilder.AddContent(seq, RenderFragment) call the
   generated Opaque path reaches through ViewRuntime.FragmentOf — this is the "hand-written
   RenderFragment" DESIGN.md §7.1 compares against. Written on one line for the same reason as
   BenchmarkViewRazor: a newline between tags would emit a markup frame the generator does not. *@
<div class="card"><h1>@Title</h1>@DynamicContent()</div>

@code {
    public string Title { get; set; } = "Benchmark";

    public int Count { get; set; }

    private RenderFragment DynamicContent() => builder => builder.AddContent(0, $"Count: {Count}");

    // Exposes the Razor compiler's render path on the same terms as OpaqueContentView.Build.
    public void Build(RenderTreeBuilder builder) => BuildRenderTree(builder);

    // Counterpart to OpaqueContentView.Invalidate; see the remarks there.
    public void Invalidate() => StateHasChanged();
}
